Probably what needs to be done is:

- during parsing, internize the TypeDecl objects. Do not link them to 
any class, obviously. Return also a SourcePos for info purposes, to be 
stored in the enclosing object. Types may keep one SourcePos as "first 
declaration".

- classes also keep a list of the types they use

- we refuse class name shadowing by template parameters, so pseudo types 
that include parameter names won't be ambiguous

- perform global analysis: sort classes and templates and index them by 
name

- for each real class iterate using numerical indexes and 
vector::size(), so that we can safely add new classes at the end during 
the iteration, go through list of types, and validate them: link type 
to actual class, or instantiate template and link to instance.

- instantiation is: duplicate the template entirely, and while doing so, 
update the list of types. When going through a type, replace 
parameters by actual value, and create new (internized) type. Add 
instance at the back of list of classes.

All Instantiate() methods take a InstMap parameter to get down deep to 
the types, and a link to the new instance, to gather all types (or just 
the type list)

Then same with extensions:

- remember current number of classes
- for all extensions, go through list of types, link them, instantiate 
the ones that are needed, put these at the back of class list
- process list from where previous stopped, (which might add new stuff 
at the back) until empty
- possibly already add the list of methods to the class

When that is done, we should have:

- all classes and instances in the list of classes, and indexed by name 
in the class map
- still templates in the template list and indexed by name, but we don't 
use them anymore
- all actual types in the type forge, linked to their class
- all fake types (types that use a template parameter) in the type 
forge, not linked to a class, and not used anymore

